    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=2,\\ b=-3,\\ c=-1,\\ d=-5$. The remaining letters satisfy $f=8-e$ and $g=20-e$; $e$ is not uniquely determined. For example, $e=1,\\ f=7,\\ g=19$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(x+5)(x+4)&=x^2+4x+5x+20\\\\&=x^2+9x+20.\\end{align*}$$\nCompare each expansion with $x^2+9x+20$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+3)(x+6)+a&=x^2+6x+3x+18+a\\\\&=x^2+9x+18+a.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $18+a=20$, so $a=2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+2)(x+10)+bx&=x^2+10x+2x+20+bx\\\\&=x^2+(12+b)x+20.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $12+b=9$, so $b=-3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+5)^2+cx+d&=x^2+5x+5x+25+cx+d\\\\&=x^2+(10+c)x+25+d.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $10+c=9$ and $25+d=20$, so $c=-1$ and $d=-5$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+1)(x+e)+fx+g&=x^2+ex+x+e+fx+g\\\\&=x^2+(e+1+f)x+e+g.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $e+1+f=9$ and $e+g=20$, giving $f=8-e$ and $g=20-e$. There are infinitely many possible triples. For example, $e=1$ gives $f=7$ and $g=19$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
